In this case, an improperly constructed format string causes the program to write beyond the bounds of allocated memory.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code overflows <code>buf</code> because, depending on the size of <code>f</code>, the format string specifier <code>&quot;%d %.1f ... &quot;</code> can exceed the amount of allocated memory.

<pre>
void formatString(int x, float f) {
   char buf[40];
   sprintf(buf, &quot;%d %.1f ... &quot;, x, f);
}
</pre>

