In this case, an improperly constructed format string causes the program to improperly convert data values or to access values outside the bounds of allocated memory.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code incorrectly converts <code>f</code> from a float using a <code>%d</code> format specifier.

<pre>
void ArgTypeMismatch(float f, int d, char *s, wchar *ws) {
   char buf[1024];
   sprintf(buf, &quot;Wrong type of %d&quot;, f);
   ...
}
</pre>

When an unsigned character cast to an integer is assign to a signed character, its value might be indistinguishable from <code>EOF</code>.

<b>Example 1:</b> The following code reads a character and compares it to <code>EOF</code>.

<pre>
char c;

while ( (c = getchar()) != '\n' &amp;&amp; c != EOF ) {
  ...
}
</pre>

In this case, the return value from <code>getchar()</code> is cast to a <code>char</code> and compared to <code>EOF</code> (an <code>int</code>). Assuming <code>c</code> is a signed 8-bit value and <code>EOF</code> is a 32-bit signed value, then if <code>getchar()</code> returns a character represented by 0xFF, the value of <code>c</code> will be sign extended to 0xFFFFFFFF in the comparison to <code>EOF</code>. Since <code>EOF</code> is typically defined as -1 (0xFFFFFFFF), the loop will terminate erroneously.

Description.Recommendations:
Do not convert the return value from character input and output functions to <code>char</code>. Instead, store them in variables of type <code>int</code>. To avoid type-conversion errors of this sort, consider using the <code>feof()</code> and <code>ferror()</code> functions instead of performing direct comparisons with <code>EOF</code>. T

<b>Example 2:</b> The following code corrects the error shown in <code>Example 1</code> using this method.

<pre>
char c;

while ( (c = getchar()) != '\n' &amp;&amp; !feof() &amp;&amp; !ferror() ) {
...
}
</pre>

Do not create illegal pointers to perform bounds checking. If you must rely on pointer arithmetic for bounds checking, cast signed pointers to their
unsigned equivalents. For example, the code in the Explanation section can be rewritten as:
<pre>
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
  char *buf;
  int len;
  ...
  len = 1&lt;&lt;30;

  if ((uintptr_t)buf+len &lt; (uintptr_t)buf) // wrap check
    [handle overflow]
</pre>

If your platform does not support <code>uintptr_t</code>, you can use <code>size_t</code> as an alternative.

Description.Explanation:
Do not explicitly deallocate stack memory. A function that defines a stack buffer will automatically deallocate the buffer when the function returns.
<Paragraph>
In this case, a stack buffer is defined in <Replace key="FirstTraceLocation.file"/> on line <Replace key="FirstTraceLocation.line"/> and freed on line <Replace key="LastTraceLocation.line"/>.
</Paragraph>
<b>Example 1:</b>
<pre>
void clean_up()
{
    char tmp[256];
    ...
    free(tmp);
    return;
}
</pre>

Explicitly freeing stack memory can corrupt memory allocation data structures. It can lead to abnormal program termination or further data corruption.

Description.Recommendations:
Do not explicitly free stack memory; allow the program to automatically reclaim it when the function returns.
<b>Example 2:</b>
<pre>
void clean_up()
{
    char tmp[256];
    ...
    /* no memory management for tmp */
    return;
}
</pre>
